@id     03_loops
@title  Loops
@topic  for loops
@lang   c

@theory
A for loop repeats code a known number of times:

    for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
        printf("%d\n", i);
    }

This prints 1, 2, 3. The three parts are: start (int i = 1), condition (i <= 3), step (i++).

To add numbers up, keep a running total in a variable that starts at 0.

Your task: read n and print the sum 1 + 2 + ... + n.
For n = 5 the answer is 15. For n = 0 the answer is 0.

@starter
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n;
    scanf("%d", &n);

    int sum = 0;
    // add 1..n to sum

    printf("%d\n", sum);
    return 0;
}

@solution
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n;
    scanf("%d", &n);
    int sum = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) sum += i;
    printf("%d\n", sum);
    return 0;
}

@test
@input
5
@expected
15

@test
@input
1
@expected
1

@hidden_test
@input
0
@expected
0

@hidden_test
@input
100
@expected
5050
